            <div xml:id="d588">

                <pb facs="0796" n="720" xml:id="pg720"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5231/1024: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-09-13T13:00:00+00:00">September 13, 1935—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 13—10:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>58. Fernandez Shaw, Chief of Commercial Agreement Section of the Ministry of
                    Commerce, yesterday informed Commercial Attaché that recent instructions to
                    Calderón with regard to passenger automobiles constitute absolute maximum Spain
                    can offer the United States. Shaw said “Our back is to the wall and we are now
                    in a position similar to the one we were in last April in our negotiations with
                    France”.</p>
                <p>In view of the fact that Spain has now stretched her offers to us to maximum
                    possible without destroying chances of commercial agreements with England and
                    France urge reconsideration points mentioned my No. 48, July 18, 2 p.m.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
